+	 * instead of scrubbing them. xfrm_output_one() sets ->ipsec_offload
+	 * and attaches the secpath (SKB_EXT_SEC_PATH); the DPAA egress driver
+	 * later reads sp->xvec[0] in dpaa_submit_outb_pkt_to_SEC() to find the
+	 * CAAM frame queue for this SA. On a PPPoE/tunnel WAN the packet
+	 * crosses a scrub boundary (e.g. ppp_start_xmit()) between that point
+	 * and the driver read, so a plain skb_ext_reset() here would drop the
+	 * secpath and the offload would silently free the packet.
+	 *
+	 * Skipping the whole reset (rather than surgically preserving just the
+	 * secpath) is safe: in this configuration the only other extension is
+	 * nf_bridge_info, which is read only by br_netfilter hooks that never
+	 * run on the egress-to-CAAM path, pins no counted netdev reference,
+	 * and is freed with the skb. br_netfilter has no IP forward/post-
+	 * routing hook to strip it, so stock already carries it out to any
+	 * non-scrubbing egress device. Every other scrub action below still
+	 * runs for offloaded skbs.
+	 */
+	if (!skb->ipsec_offload)
+#endif
+		skb_ext_reset(skb);
 	nf_reset_ct(skb);
 	nf_reset_trace(skb);
 
